WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:25.120
Hello, everyone.

9
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:26.580
Hello.

10
00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:30.120
Hello and to love's eternal glory.

11
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:40.740
Oh, lady, we should tell people this is the first podcast that we are recording in 2022.

12
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:45.800
The other ones you've been listening to this year we recorded before the new year.

13
00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.720
This is our first one back after our vacations.

14
00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:50.460
We are back.

15
00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:52.780
We are semi-rested.

16
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.760
We're also semi-back.

17
00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.060
I am back in my closet.

18
00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:58.920
You are.

19
00:00:58.920 --> 00:00:59.840
You are in the studio.

20
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.760
Oh, guys, there are some stricter protocols here at the beginning of 2022.

21
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.660
So I let Angela have the studio.

22
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:09.820
Me and Sam.

23
00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:10.560
What's up, Sam?

24
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:11.400
Hi, everybody.

25
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:12.120
Aw.

26
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.180
Hey, Sam!

27
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.900
Cassie and I are over here on Zoom.

28
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:16.900
Hi, ladies.

29
00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:17.620
Hi.

30
00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:24.180
I will point out, you guys, Jenna, is that a ginormous plant in your podcast closet?

31
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:25.380
It is.

32
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.140
Do you notice Cassie's got a plant over her shoulder as well?

33
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.560
Well, I guess when you podcast from home, you need a plant.

34
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:34.800
That's totally a coincidence.

35
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:36.780
Sam has a little Christmas tree.

36
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:37.720
Damn it.

37
00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:39.620
Lady, are you the only one without a plant?

38
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:40.840
I'm plantless.

39
00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:44.000
Which is so crazy because you're the plant tracker.

40
00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:45.820
I love plants, guys.

41
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.440
I guess I'm going to have to start traveling with my own plant.

42
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:49.420
I'm going to be that lady.

43
00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.800
You know how you see people that travel always with a pet?

44
00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.720
Maybe I'm going to be the lady that's like, hey, do you see that lady with the little tiny

45
00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:59.060
succulent in her lap on the plane?

46
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:06.000
I would love to travel with you as old ladies where you just clutch a tiny succulent the

47
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:06.540
whole time.

48
00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:07.920
That's amazing.

49
00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:11.640
You guys, I should share with you that for Christmas, Jenna and I exchanged gifts.

50
00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:17.520
I got her some cozy pajama pants and she got me a succulent throw pillow.

51
00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.480
It looks like a giant succulent, but it's a cozy pillow.

52
00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:22.640
It really does.

53
00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.560
I brought it home and Isabel said, mom, is that pillow a succulent?

54
00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.580
And I was like, yes, yes, it is, Isabel.

55
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:29.800
Oh my God.

56
00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:32.880
You guys, Sam just brought me a plant from the lobby.

57
00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:35.620
Get a picture.

58
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:39.620
Oh, Cassie, you need to get in this picture with your plant.

59
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.460
Okay, Sam, you hold up your tree.

60
00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:42.900
Ready?

61
00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:43.540
Here we go.

62
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:44.520
One, two, three.

63
00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:46.720
That is amazing.

64
00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:52.560
Well, you know, listen, we might have some protocols that keep us apart, but we're enjoying

65
00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:53.440
plants together.

66
00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:54.900
You guys, that's something.

67
00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:55.900
It's something.

68
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:56.740
It's huge.

69
00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.000
I'll take it.

70
00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.140
Should we get into this episode?

71
00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:03.240
We really should, because there's a lot of fun stuff to discuss.

72
00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:05.260
This is The Promotion.

73
00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.740
It is season six, episode three.

74
00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:14.520
It is written by Jen Salata, directed by Jen Salata, and here is your summary.

75
00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:21.740
Michael and Jim's managerial power struggle intensifies when David Wallace reveals that

76
00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:26.000
they don't have enough money to give everyone in the office their yearly raises.

77
00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:32.260
So he leaves it up to the co-managers to determine who gets a raise.

78
00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:39.080
Meanwhile, Pam tries to get people to give her money instead of traditional wedding gifts.

79
00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.680
That storyline really cracked me up.

80
00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:44.200
It did me too.

81
00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:45.320
All right.

82
00:03:45.500 --> 00:03:46.900
Fast fact number one.

83
00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:49.100
I have a location breakdown.

84
00:03:49.460 --> 00:03:51.000
I thought you would.

85
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:54.980
You might be thinking, wait, they didn't go anywhere on location.

86
00:03:54.980 --> 00:03:59.000
Please tell me your location breakdown is Jim's glass cube.

87
00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:00.340
It is, lady.

88
00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:01.400
Is it really?

89
00:04:03.030 --> 00:04:07.610
Well, we got a bunch of fan questions, including from Tara Jade L.

90
00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.390
in South Australia.

91
00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:13.330
Can we please talk about the layout of the office changing?

92
00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.050
Because I don't think it's ever mentioned.

93
00:04:15.230 --> 00:04:19.410
A new office just appears for Jim to move into that was never there before.

94
00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:22.390
And from Seoul C.

95
00:04:22.630 --> 00:04:26.510
in Taiwan, what was involved in making Jim's office?

96
00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:30.270
Did they break down the wall behind Kevin's desk to make space?

97
00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:31.690
And Molly S.

98
00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.170
from Arkansas says, how long did this process take?

99
00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:36.990
Did it slow down the shooting at all?

100
00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:38.530
See, lots of questions.

101
00:04:38.530 --> 00:04:39.710
Lots of questions.

102
00:04:40.110 --> 00:04:42.470
I'm going to have to agree with Tara Jade.

103
00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:43.890
It's true.

104
00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:49.230
Jim is just suddenly in a tiny glass cube in the office and no one says anything.

105
00:04:49.690 --> 00:04:51.750
No one says, where did this come from?

106
00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:53.210
I know.

107
00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:57.730
I would have loved just one talking head where someone was like, what the hell?

108
00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:58.590
I know.

109
00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:00.990
Like, you know who would have been perfect is Ryan.

110
00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:02.470
Ryan being like, what the hell?

111
00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.470
Jim's glass box took over the whole corner of the office.

112
00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:07.510
I agree.

113
00:05:07.510 --> 00:05:13.710
Well, Jim's office was actually a pretty simple addition that was installed over the weekend.

114
00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:21.410
Randy Cordray shared with us that this is because we were able to build all the walls in our scenery mill,

115
00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.430
which was across the street from our studio lot.

116
00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:27.230
Michael Gallenberg designed them.

117
00:05:27.410 --> 00:05:30.370
They were built by our construction coordinator, Tim James.

118
00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:33.230
And then the walls were installed pretty quickly.

119
00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:39.930
They did arrange our shoot schedule so that we didn't shoot inside Jim's office until later in the week.

120
00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:44.770
This was mostly so that set dressing could decorate it.

121
00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:45.430
Right.

122
00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:51.030
I did zoom in to see if the glass walls went all the way to our fakie ceiling.

123
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:51.670
And?

124
00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:52.570
And they did.

125
00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:53.150
Oh!

126
00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:56.730
The top part is frosted, like these frosted rectangles.

127
00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:00.110
And then the bottom part is clear glass with blinds.

128
00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:01.070
Wow.

129
00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:06.110
Well, Randy mentioned that the new office was kind of right behind Creed's area.

130
00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:09.830
They didn't have to modify anything that was existing.

131
00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:10.970
It just kind of fit.

132
00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:12.290
That's what she said.

133
00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:18.450
There was one piece of set dressing that Randy pointed out that he says you can't see in the episode,

134
00:06:18.450 --> 00:06:19.770
but he absolutely loves it.

135
00:06:19.830 --> 00:06:23.970
He sent us a picture of it, which is Jim's diploma.

136
00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:26.330
What's it say?

137
00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:29.590
It's just, you know, that he graduated from college.

138
00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:32.810
It was created by our graphics designer, Ryan Cosgrove.

139
00:06:32.930 --> 00:06:35.530
So we can post that in Office Ladies Stories.

140
00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:36.750
Oh, fun.

141
00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.810
That was your breakdown of Jim's Cube.

142
00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:42.630
Well, you know, I noticed a few things in Jim's Cube.

143
00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:43.610
Oh, yeah?

144
00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:44.330
Mm-hmm.

145
00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.690
I noticed a calendar, which seems important.

146
00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:50.330
But did you see the giant poster of a train?

147
00:06:50.930 --> 00:06:51.370
Yeah.

148
00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:52.750
What's that?

149
00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:56.770
And then I thought, is there a train club at Dunder Mifflin we don't know about?

150
00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:58.730
I mean, Dwight and Toby love trains.

151
00:06:58.730 --> 00:06:59.910
We learned that in the meeting.

152
00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:02.910
Is there a train, Finer's thing club?

153
00:07:03.390 --> 00:07:04.570
Oh, my gosh.

154
00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:05.570
A train club.

155
00:07:05.570 --> 00:07:06.190
I don't know.

156
00:07:06.470 --> 00:07:09.890
These are now three people at Dunder Mifflin that clearly have a thing for trains.

157
00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:10.630
I'm just saying.

158
00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:11.370
I see a theme.

159
00:07:11.630 --> 00:07:13.250
I took a picture of the train.

160
00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:14.890
I zoomed in.

161
00:07:15.010 --> 00:07:15.670
I couldn't make out.

162
00:07:15.790 --> 00:07:18.210
There's some kind of, like, saying underneath the train.

163
00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:20.110
I really wish I could have made that out.

164
00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:22.510
But if you guys caught it, let us know.

165
00:07:22.870 --> 00:07:29.130
I just want to say, Ange, I feel like maybe you're the person who has a thing for trains.

166
00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:30.490
No, I don't.

167
00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:34.350
You zoomed in on a picture of a train to see what model it was.

168
00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:37.690
And then you Googled all the models of the trains from the meeting.

169
00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:42.670
I just feel like maybe you're projecting, do you love trains?

170
00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:44.430
Listen, first of all.

171
00:07:44.550 --> 00:07:45.110
Dig deep.

172
00:07:45.350 --> 00:07:46.190
Really think hard.

173
00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:47.710
First of all, how dare you?

174
00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:48.970
And yes, I do love trains.

175
00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.850
Secondly, whenever I see a train on a road trip, I get really excited.

176
00:07:53.030 --> 00:07:54.230
And I point it out to the kids.

177
00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:55.110
I say, look, train.

178
00:07:55.410 --> 00:07:56.190
Look at the train.

179
00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:59.510
My uncle was a conductor of a train.

180
00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:01.390
Lady, this is a breakthrough.

181
00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:03.770
But come on.

182
00:08:03.930 --> 00:08:08.010
Jim has one poster in his whole little glass cube, and it's of a train.

183
00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:09.670
Of course I'm going to notice that.

184
00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:13.710
I mean, I just assumed it was, like, some leftover poster in storage.

185
00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:14.790
And he was like, sure.

186
00:08:15.630 --> 00:08:16.330
You're right.

187
00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:16.730
You're right.

188
00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:17.190
I don't know.

189
00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:19.310
Jim is probably not attached to it at all.

190
00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:23.270
I'm going to get you a picture of a train next year for Christmas.

191
00:08:23.610 --> 00:08:24.310
Are you really?

192
00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:24.930
Maybe.

193
00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:27.410
You know what I've always wanted to do?

194
00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:28.010
What?

195
00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:29.850
Is take the train.

196
00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:30.890
Through Scotland?

197
00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:31.750
Yes!

198
00:08:32.050 --> 00:08:32.490
Yes.

199
00:08:32.790 --> 00:08:34.090
I want to do that.

200
00:08:34.310 --> 00:08:39.290
I want to do that, too, but I'm afraid I won't sleep well, and I'll just, like, be grouchy the whole trip.

201
00:08:39.430 --> 00:08:41.210
You know I don't do good without sleep.

202
00:08:41.450 --> 00:08:43.210
Lady, the Queen of England does it.

203
00:08:43.430 --> 00:08:44.370
You can do it.

204
00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:47.390
The Queen of England has a fancy train car.

205
00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:48.810
Well, that's true.

206
00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:52.530
Would I take the Queen of England's train car through Scotland?

207
00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:53.070
Sure.

208
00:08:56.830 --> 00:08:57.890
I want to go.

209
00:08:58.070 --> 00:08:58.670
I want to do it.

210
00:08:58.730 --> 00:08:59.490
You have to do it with me.

211
00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:00.350
All right, I will.

212
00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:02.710
You and your little succulent can ride the train.

213
00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:06.110
Thank you, because I'll jump in something icy with you at the end.

214
00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:06.850
Yay!

215
00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:07.750
This is great.

216
00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:08.410
Done.

217
00:09:08.410 --> 00:09:11.170
And then we can make a Hallmark movie about it.

218
00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:14.570
Two ladies in a succulent traveling through Scotland.

219
00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:17.090
To jump in an icy cold pond.

220
00:09:17.630 --> 00:09:18.310
Tune in!

221
00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:20.470
This is a Hallmark movie.

222
00:09:21.170 --> 00:09:23.230
It's going to go with laundry and betrayal.

223
00:09:23.830 --> 00:09:24.590
Oh, God.

224
00:09:24.590 --> 00:09:26.190
I really want that to happen.

225
00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:28.050
We should really be producers.

226
00:09:28.890 --> 00:09:29.690
All right.

227
00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:32.130
I'm going to move us on to fast fact number two.

228
00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:35.270
We should run a network for boring middle-aged women.

229
00:09:39.900 --> 00:09:41.300
Okay, move us on.

230
00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:41.980
Move us on.

231
00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:43.700
Fast fact number two.

232
00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:47.940
This episode was originally titled Co-Managers.

233
00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:54.040
It aired on October 1st, 2009, when it was retitled The Promotion.

234
00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:55.680
Here's something crazy.

235
00:09:55.840 --> 00:09:58.020
I was in a movie called The Promotion.

236
00:09:58.020 --> 00:09:59.140
I know!

237
00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:01.860
It was released in 2008.

238
00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:04.320
It starred John C.

239
00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:08.780
Reilly and Sean William Scott as these two guys who are dueling for

240
00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:11.200
the same job as a grocery store manager.

241
00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.000
We shot it in Chicago.

242
00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:15.860
You know what I had to do in order to shoot that movie?

243
00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:20.040
I would shoot on The Office all during the week, Monday through Friday.

244
00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:24.640
I would get on an airplane, take the red eye to Chicago, sleep on the plane.

245
00:10:24.640 --> 00:10:30.940
I would work Saturday and Sunday on the movie, take the red eye back to L.A., and just go

246
00:10:30.940 --> 00:10:32.080
to work on The Office.

247
00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:38.240
One morning on a Monday after I had done this, I was in a wardrobe fitting, and I fell asleep

248
00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:38.940
standing up.

249
00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:40.460
I was so tired.

250
00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:41.680
It was insane.

251
00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:45.900
That's the kind of stuff you can do when you're young, and I couldn't do that today.

252
00:10:46.120 --> 00:10:46.860
There's no way.

253
00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:48.740
Lady, guess what?

254
00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:49.260
What?

255
00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.760
I remembered that you were in a movie called The Promotion, and I looked through my

256
00:10:52.760 --> 00:10:54.440
digital clutter, and guess what I found?

257
00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:55.000
What?

258
00:10:55.300 --> 00:10:56.620
I went to your premiere.

259
00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:58.020
Oh, yeah.

260
00:10:58.380 --> 00:11:01.820
And not only did I go, Ed Helms and I carpooled.

261
00:11:02.140 --> 00:11:03.320
Oh, my gosh.

262
00:11:03.620 --> 00:11:07.920
I found our emails back and forth about, like, who would pick up who or who could drive

263
00:11:07.920 --> 00:11:10.140
who, and Ed said, all good.

264
00:11:10.140 --> 00:11:11.360
He came and picked me up.

265
00:11:11.420 --> 00:11:15.580
He said, let me know what time, and I'll pick you up, and maybe I can meet Isabel.

266
00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.060
That is so sweet.

267
00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.140
Because Isabel was only a few weeks old.

268
00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.400
Your premiere was May 28th, lady.

269
00:11:22.760 --> 00:11:24.560
And she was born May 3rd.

270
00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:29.860
You came to my movie premiere just a few weeks after your baby was born?

271
00:11:30.160 --> 00:11:30.620
Yes.

272
00:11:30.840 --> 00:11:34.600
This is something I did not appreciate at the time because I was not a mother yet.

273
00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:40.040
Well, I looked to see if there were any photos, and in our emails, Ed and I were meeting up

274
00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:44.360
with Rachel Harris, and we were all going to go, and I found photos of Rachel Harris.

275
00:11:44.440 --> 00:11:47.860
I found photos of Melora, Oscar, and Kate all at your premiere.

276
00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.040
Not a single photo of me and Ed.

277
00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:50.880
Oh, no.

278
00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:53.220
I don't know if we were running late.

279
00:11:53.400 --> 00:11:55.980
I don't know if because I had just had a baby.

280
00:11:56.120 --> 00:11:58.160
I was like, yeah, no, I'm not walking that red carpet.

281
00:11:58.900 --> 00:12:01.120
But we did go, and we carpooled.

282
00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:03.260
Oh, well, I really appreciated that.

283
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:04.580
I loved being in that movie.

284
00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:09.480
I made a really good friend on that movie, and Fred Armisen, he had another supporting

285
00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:09.740
role.

286
00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:11.280
So we had a lot of downtime together.

287
00:12:11.420 --> 00:12:12.300
We hung out a lot.

288
00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:16.800
And he was one of the people like you who really took care of me in New York when I broke my

289
00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:17.180
back.

290
00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:20.060
And yeah, that was, I loved that movie.

291
00:12:20.440 --> 00:12:21.480
I loved it, too.

292
00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:24.800
Fred is one of the funniest people I've ever met in my life.

293
00:12:24.960 --> 00:12:27.180
And Jenna, you and I have worked with a lot of funny people.

294
00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.060
So that is saying a lot.

295
00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:29.600
Yeah.

296
00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:36.380
But I remember one time coming over and hanging out with you and Fred, and I was crying.

297
00:12:36.380 --> 00:12:37.240
I was laughing.

298
00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:40.060
Like, I was laughing so hard, I couldn't catch my breath.

299
00:12:40.220 --> 00:12:45.320
Just at him, just riffing about, I don't know what we were talking about, just random stuff.

300
00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.700
But he's so flippin' funny.

301
00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:48.340
No.

302
00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:50.020
Him and Chris Parnell.

303
00:12:50.580 --> 00:12:51.760
Oh, forget it.

304
00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:52.500
Forget it.

305
00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:53.400
But I can't.

306
00:12:53.460 --> 00:12:53.880
Yeah.

307
00:12:54.020 --> 00:12:54.720
It's too much.

308
00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:55.780
Ah.

309
00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:00.180
Chris Parnell and Fred Armisen, will you be in our movie where we travel with a succulent

310
00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:00.800
through Scotland?

311
00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:02.560
We would just laugh the whole time.

312
00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:03.400
It would be wonderful.

313
00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:04.920
I want that experience.

314
00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:06.500
All right.

315
00:13:06.660 --> 00:13:09.460
Are you ready for fast fact number three?

316
00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:10.460
Yes.

317
00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.260
Jen Salata, as I said, was our writer-director.

318
00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:22.040
And I reached out to her because I noticed that this episode is basically just one big A

319
00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:24.920
story, like, including the cold open.

320
00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:27.540
It's not even like a standalone cold open.

321
00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:29.700
It's an on-plot cold open.

322
00:13:29.840 --> 00:13:30.220
Yeah.

323
00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:32.360
It's one big story start to finish.

324
00:13:32.860 --> 00:13:34.340
There's no B story.

325
00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:38.040
There's this little bitty runner about Pam and her registry.

326
00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:41.340
But here's what Jen had to say about that.

327
00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:46.020
I think because it was a bigger story for Michael and Jim and a story which affected

328
00:13:46.020 --> 00:13:49.240
everyone in the office, we opted for no B story on this one.

329
00:13:49.240 --> 00:13:53.660
But I noticed when I rewatched it that a lot of the how it affected everyone in the office

330
00:13:53.660 --> 00:13:54.920
didn't make it into the episode.

331
00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:59.100
There were some really fun talking heads about who everyone preferred as a boss, Michael

332
00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:01.020
or Jim, but they didn't make it in.

333
00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:03.640
I love the small money runner for Pam.

334
00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:06.540
The moment you see your new last name on the check was my favorite.

335
00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:11.060
I like how different it felt in this episode to see Jim in his own office.

336
00:14:11.380 --> 00:14:15.260
You'd imagine Jim having his own office would be a nice thing, but then it separates him

337
00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:18.200
out from the others in a way that Michael has also separated out.

338
00:14:18.440 --> 00:14:21.080
So there's definitely good and bad in this promotion.

339
00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:26.680
I always love when writers speak thematically about an episode.

340
00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:28.700
I'm real nerdy like that.

341
00:14:28.900 --> 00:14:30.720
I just love how smart they are.

342
00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:33.920
And I just loved it.

343
00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:34.640
I know.

344
00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:37.080
I'm just like, ha ha ha, this is so funny.

345
00:14:37.280 --> 00:14:40.620
And they're like, I'm going to break down how this comedy works.

346
00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:42.480
Yeah, exactly.

347
00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:44.180
Well, Jen is right.

348
00:14:44.400 --> 00:14:45.980
There are a lot of deleted scenes.

349
00:14:46.040 --> 00:14:47.460
I really enjoyed them.

350
00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:50.340
I'm going to share a few later in the episode where they would have come.

351
00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:53.720
But there's maybe just a little teaser here.

352
00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:56.860
My favorite Stanley talking head ever that got deleted.

353
00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:58.900
Oh, it's going to come later.

354
00:14:59.380 --> 00:15:03.840
But yeah, if you have the DVDs, go to the deleted scenes for this one because they are

355
00:15:03.840 --> 00:15:04.360
delicious.

356
00:15:05.080 --> 00:15:06.620
Well, lady, that's all I've got.

357
00:15:06.820 --> 00:15:09.760
I think we could take a break and then we'll get into it.

358
00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:10.900
Let's do it.

359
00:15:10.900 --> 00:15:21.330
All right.

360
00:15:21.610 --> 00:15:26.650
This episode starts with Dwight needing a signature for an expense report from Michael.

361
00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:28.150
But Michael says, you know what?

362
00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:30.710
That doesn't fall under his category of boss, right?

363
00:15:30.790 --> 00:15:31.630
He's big picture.

364
00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:33.130
And he sends him to Jim.

365
00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:37.650
And poor Dwight now is a ping pong ball going back and forth between these two guys.

366
00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:38.510
Yeah.

367
00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:42.390
Jim is kind of like, hmm, I don't know.

368
00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:44.850
I'll sign it, but only if you say please.

369
00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:48.350
So then Dwight tries to file a report on Jim.

370
00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:50.610
Oh, it's a mess.

371
00:15:50.750 --> 00:15:52.190
This co-manager thing is a mess.

372
00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:53.570
It's already a mess.

373
00:15:53.850 --> 00:15:56.870
Dwight has a talking head where he shares what he'd like to do to Jim.

374
00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:58.650
Put him in a triangle choke hold.

375
00:15:59.110 --> 00:15:59.870
Force him down.

376
00:16:00.330 --> 00:16:04.370
He basically, Dwight wants to be like a professional wrestler, right?

377
00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:05.410
Is that what's happening here?

378
00:16:05.510 --> 00:16:06.770
That's what he's describing.

379
00:16:06.770 --> 00:16:07.170
Yeah.

380
00:16:07.170 --> 00:16:11.970
Well, I have a couple of observations about this cold open.

381
00:16:12.310 --> 00:16:12.690
Okay.

382
00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:16.130
Number one, Jim is suited up.

383
00:16:16.270 --> 00:16:16.870
Well, yeah.

384
00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:19.630
He's got his ambition jacket back on again.

385
00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:21.550
He's super serious.

386
00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:22.410
Mm-hmm.

387
00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:23.390
He's boss man.

388
00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:24.290
He's boss.

389
00:16:25.630 --> 00:16:28.450
Dwight is not wearing mustard.

390
00:16:28.810 --> 00:16:29.830
Did you notice?

391
00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:31.250
Oh, my God.

392
00:16:31.390 --> 00:16:31.630
Yeah.

393
00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:33.650
I mean, I didn't, but now I, yeah.

394
00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:40.230
He's also in a full suit, and his shirt is like this blue-gray-green color.

395
00:16:40.770 --> 00:16:41.710
Very interesting.

396
00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:43.990
Because he's vying for the position.

397
00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:44.930
Perhaps.

398
00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:46.690
So he has to look serious.

399
00:16:47.910 --> 00:16:53.010
Another observation about this cold open actually comes from our writer-director, Jen Salata,

400
00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:57.010
and she talks a little bit about what it was like to shoot.

401
00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:58.230
Here's what she said.

402
00:16:58.850 --> 00:17:02.870
I always really respected the directors that would play by the two-camera doc rule that

403
00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:04.050
was established early on.

404
00:17:04.530 --> 00:17:07.690
Many did, but I really remember Jeff Blitz always sticking to that rule.

405
00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:11.690
There were so many good and beautiful episodes where you could feel the presence of more than

406
00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:15.590
two cameras, but I loved when directors would have to reach to get what they wanted using

407
00:17:15.590 --> 00:17:16.330
only the two.

408
00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:21.690
I tried hard to figure out a way to lose the third camera in that cold open when Dwight

409
00:17:21.690 --> 00:17:25.650
was running back and forth between Michael and Jim's office, but I never cracked it, and

410
00:17:25.650 --> 00:17:26.830
it still kind of bugs me.

411
00:17:28.130 --> 00:17:28.990
It's so funny.

412
00:17:29.090 --> 00:17:29.990
I didn't even notice.

413
00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:37.490
I didn't either, but I do remember Jeff Blitz really trying to stick to a traditional documentary.

414
00:17:37.810 --> 00:17:39.430
I remember that about him.

415
00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:41.410
Well, he was a documentarian.

416
00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:42.030
Yeah.

417
00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:48.070
You know, so that makes sense to me, and I know that Jen worked really closely with him,

418
00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:52.030
so it also makes sense to me that she would want to adhere to it as well.

419
00:17:52.330 --> 00:17:52.630
Yeah.

420
00:17:52.630 --> 00:17:58.230
Well, I guess if you re-watch that cold open, you can look for the three camera angles instead

421
00:17:58.230 --> 00:18:02.690
of two, but I have to say, Jen, I didn't see it, so you tricked me.

422
00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:03.990
You got us.

423
00:18:04.310 --> 00:18:08.310
So now we're going to get into this episode, and Michael wants everyone to gather in the

424
00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:09.190
conference room.

425
00:18:09.190 --> 00:18:14.030
He wants to have a meeting, and he suggests that everyone bring a snack because this meeting

426
00:18:14.030 --> 00:18:14.850
might take a while.

427
00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:15.890
Oh, Lord.

428
00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:19.310
And Jim is like, Michael, can I speak to you in my office?

429
00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:23.210
And Michael is like, maybe you can come speak to me in my office.

430
00:18:23.490 --> 00:18:26.250
And then Stanley says, wait, can I be a boss too?

431
00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:27.370
Yes.

432
00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:32.170
And then Oscar has a talking head where he says it doesn't take a genius to know that

433
00:18:32.170 --> 00:18:34.890
an organization thrives with two leaders.

434
00:18:36.110 --> 00:18:41.790
Lady, I looked up some organizations that have two CEOs.

435
00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:42.710
Oh.

436
00:18:42.710 --> 00:18:44.250
And guess what?

437
00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:46.230
Not the Popes, I'm guessing.

438
00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:47.970
No, not the Popes.

439
00:18:48.090 --> 00:18:48.570
Who?

440
00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:49.830
Netflix.

441
00:18:50.590 --> 00:18:51.150
Okay.

442
00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:52.550
Warby Parker.

443
00:18:53.230 --> 00:18:53.450
Oh.

444
00:18:53.990 --> 00:18:54.650
Salesforce.

445
00:18:55.590 --> 00:19:00.010
There's a very successful meal delivery service called Fresh and Lean.

446
00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:05.490
And most notably, Ramble, our company.

447
00:19:05.770 --> 00:19:06.130
Woohoo!

448
00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:13.180
And by the way, our organization is thriving with two leaders.

449
00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:15.360
Oscar, you got it wrong.

450
00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:17.160
We are doing great.

451
00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:23.060
So this really starts a whole bunch of deleted scenes that Jen was talking about.

452
00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:29.040
One of them involves Kevin, just in the fact that he doesn't know how to relate to Jim

453
00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:30.060
anymore as a boss.

454
00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:31.560
Jim was his buddy, right?

455
00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:33.340
And it's really funny.

456
00:19:33.340 --> 00:19:36.760
He ends up oversharing a whole bunch of things he shouldn't be saying to a boss.

457
00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:37.640
We have to hear it.

458
00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:41.980
I just wanted to congratulate you on your promotion.

459
00:19:42.220 --> 00:19:42.740
Oh, thanks.

460
00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:43.200
Yeah.

461
00:19:46.690 --> 00:19:55.290
Also, I was wondering if you could forget what I said last week about the fact that I don't

462
00:19:55.290 --> 00:19:56.610
really do my job.

463
00:19:57.110 --> 00:19:57.830
Oh, sure.

464
00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:00.030
Is that why you've been avoiding me?

465
00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:00.950
Oh, no.

466
00:20:01.170 --> 00:20:02.070
I wouldn't do that.

467
00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:08.630
Oh, it was just that I was late today, and I took a nap in the bathroom for a while.

468
00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:09.350
Oh, right.

469
00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:12.010
I think you're going to want to stop telling me those things.

470
00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:14.070
Gotcha.

471
00:20:14.510 --> 00:20:16.670
I will stop telling you things.

472
00:20:17.810 --> 00:20:19.730
Aw, that kind of breaks my heart.

473
00:20:19.910 --> 00:20:20.610
I know.

474
00:20:21.230 --> 00:20:24.790
But also, how often is Kevin napping in the bathroom?

475
00:20:25.850 --> 00:20:26.330
Exactly.

476
00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:32.070
So Jim ultimately decides, I guess, he will go into Michael's office, right?

477
00:20:32.210 --> 00:20:34.030
He won't make Michael come into his.

478
00:20:34.970 --> 00:20:40.630
And right when he sits down to talk to Michael, I caught a little background surfing the internet.

479
00:20:40.970 --> 00:20:41.830
Oh, my gosh.

480
00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:42.410
Me, too.

481
00:20:42.610 --> 00:20:44.230
It's at 4 minutes, 12 seconds.

482
00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:48.190
It's also at 3 minutes, 34 seconds, and it's Rainn Wilson.

483
00:20:48.850 --> 00:20:51.870
Oh, I caught myself scrolling the internet.

484
00:20:52.150 --> 00:20:52.590
Did you really?

485
00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:53.330
Yes.

486
00:20:53.350 --> 00:20:57.210
I caught Rainn, and you guys are both surfing the internet during this whole scene.

487
00:20:57.490 --> 00:20:58.350
We really are.

488
00:20:58.710 --> 00:20:59.730
That's hilarious.

489
00:21:00.410 --> 00:21:04.770
This is the scene where Michael calls Jim, Jim James, Jimothy.

490
00:21:05.290 --> 00:21:05.650
Mm-hmm.

491
00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:07.510
He's like, oh, that just sounds weird.

492
00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:08.730
Can I go back to Jim?

493
00:21:10.010 --> 00:21:15.830
So Jimothy is explaining that these conference room meetings are eating up, like, a large part of the day.

494
00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:16.890
They are very disruptive.

495
00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:18.550
And Michael's like, you know what else?

496
00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:20.570
Eats up a large part of the day, napping.

497
00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:21.430
Mm.

498
00:21:21.610 --> 00:21:23.850
You fall asleep, you wake up, and it's evening.

499
00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:24.790
Yeah.

500
00:21:25.130 --> 00:21:26.250
So I'll tell you what, Angela.

501
00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:27.530
This is a delightful scene.

502
00:21:27.610 --> 00:21:28.430
It's very funny.

503
00:21:28.790 --> 00:21:34.190
But all I noticed in this scene was the blooming plant behind Jim.

504
00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:36.330
Yes, I did, too.

505
00:21:37.190 --> 00:21:38.390
I had to Google it.

506
00:21:38.590 --> 00:21:40.350
I had to know, what is this plant?

507
00:21:40.550 --> 00:21:41.430
You know what it is?

508
00:21:41.670 --> 00:21:42.370
Yes, I do.

509
00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:45.830
It is a peace lily, a.k.a.

510
00:21:46.530 --> 00:21:47.930
the closet plant.

511
00:21:49.310 --> 00:21:54.350
I found out it is very popular in homes and offices because they don't need a ton of light

512
00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:55.110
or water.

513
00:21:55.750 --> 00:22:03.730
So when I realized I would be podcasting in my closet this week, I got myself a peace lily.

514
00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:05.450
That's my plant that you noticed.

515
00:22:05.690 --> 00:22:06.670
Oh, I did notice.

516
00:22:06.770 --> 00:22:10.910
Now, it's not blooming because if it's blooming, it's got, it's white with like a little yellow

517
00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:11.370
thing.

518
00:22:11.550 --> 00:22:12.930
And that's how you can tell.

