721
00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:55.300
Also, we learned that Michael hates a pros and cons liSt.

722
00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:56.600
Oh, my gosh.

723
00:30:57.320 --> 00:30:57.960
Hates it.

724
00:30:58.460 --> 00:30:59.660
Absolutely hates it.

725
00:30:59.660 --> 00:31:04.000
And then he has that amazing quote where he says, sometimes the smartest people don't

726
00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:04.640
think at all.

727
00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:08.440
Well, lady, I had to know.

728
00:31:08.580 --> 00:31:09.460
I don't know why.

729
00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:15.340
I just, on the fly, decided to Google who invented the pros and cons liSt.

730
00:31:15.580 --> 00:31:16.540
Did you really?

731
00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:18.800
The answer is fascinating.

732
00:31:19.160 --> 00:31:19.960
Oh, my gosh.

733
00:31:19.960 --> 00:31:20.840
I want to hear it.

734
00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:23.800
There is an inventor of the pros and cons liSt.

735
00:31:23.920 --> 00:31:26.200
And it is Ben Franklin.

736
00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:27.280
What?

737
00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:28.880
Ben Franklin.

738
00:31:29.320 --> 00:31:29.960
All right.

739
00:31:30.140 --> 00:31:33.160
This is where everyone thinks the pros and cons list came from.

740
00:31:33.480 --> 00:31:41.300
He wrote a letter in 1772 to Joseph Priestley, where he described in great detail how he

741
00:31:41.300 --> 00:31:42.220
makes a decision.

742
00:31:42.860 --> 00:31:44.420
I'm going to read it to you.

743
00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:46.440
Every word fascinated me.

744
00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:48.180
He said, quote,

745
00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:53.720
quote, my way, he capitalized way, by the way, his way.

746
00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:54.460
Okay.

747
00:31:55.000 --> 00:31:56.800
Frank Sinatra, we hear you.

748
00:31:57.480 --> 00:32:05.440
Is to divide a half sheet of paper by a line into two columns, writing over the one pro and

749
00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:06.940
over the other con.

750
00:32:06.940 --> 00:32:16.260
Then, during three or four days of consideration, I put down under the different headings, different

751
00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:21.100
motives that at different times occur to me for or against the measure.

752
00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:27.940
When I have thus got them all together in one view, I endeavor to estimate their respective

753
00:32:27.940 --> 00:32:28.440
weights.

754
00:32:28.960 --> 00:32:34.000
Where I find two, one on each side that seem equal, I strike them both out.

755
00:32:34.640 --> 00:32:40.940
If I find a reason pro that is equal to two reasons con, I'll strike out all three.

756
00:32:41.800 --> 00:32:49.580
If I judge two reasons con equal to three reasons pro, I strike out all five, and thus proceed

757
00:32:49.580 --> 00:32:52.000
until the balance lies.

758
00:32:52.440 --> 00:32:58.340
And if after a day or two of further consideration, nothing new of importance occurs on either

759
00:32:58.340 --> 00:33:01.620
side, I can come to a determination accordingly.

760
00:33:02.120 --> 00:33:06.460
Then he said, I have found great advantage from this kind of equation.

761
00:33:06.460 --> 00:33:12.240
And he said, I believe I have discovered what could be called moral or prudential algebra.

762
00:33:12.800 --> 00:33:17.020
He believes that he had found a mathematical equation for making a decision.

763
00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:21.400
Well, I can tell you right now, he would have driven Michael Scott crazy.

764
00:33:21.820 --> 00:33:22.260
Crazy.

765
00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:26.200
If he walked in and said, listen, it's going to take me three days.

766
00:33:26.540 --> 00:33:26.840
Yeah.

767
00:33:27.020 --> 00:33:30.120
With this pros and cons list to figure out what we're going to do.

768
00:33:30.300 --> 00:33:31.500
Michael would have been like, I'm out.

769
00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:36.000
Well, when I read this, I also realized I've been doing pros and cons lists.

770
00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:37.020
Wrong, I guess.

771
00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:42.420
I just make lists of pros and cons, and then I can see them all in one place.

772
00:33:42.820 --> 00:33:48.160
I didn't realize I was supposed to strike them out if I consider them to be of equal importance.

773
00:33:48.840 --> 00:33:52.880
I didn't realize there was a whole striking out process that Ben Franklin intended.

774
00:33:53.660 --> 00:33:54.980
Well, Jenna, I know you love a liSt.

775
00:33:55.240 --> 00:33:59.020
I have no doubt you've made many pro and con lists in your life.

776
00:33:59.340 --> 00:33:59.760
True, true.

777
00:34:00.440 --> 00:34:05.820
Well, I'll have you know, Angela, next Jim is going to make a pie chart of Michael's time.

778
00:34:06.000 --> 00:34:06.680
Mm-hmm.

779
00:34:06.820 --> 00:34:08.120
How Michael spends time.

780
00:34:08.500 --> 00:34:08.720
Yeah.

781
00:34:08.960 --> 00:34:11.280
It looks like he used crayon, by the way.

782
00:34:11.580 --> 00:34:12.260
I know.

783
00:34:12.540 --> 00:34:14.860
I also Googled who invented the pie chart.

784
00:34:15.180 --> 00:34:16.180
Did you really?

785
00:34:16.720 --> 00:34:17.040
Yeah.

786
00:34:17.540 --> 00:34:18.560
Who did it?

787
00:34:18.880 --> 00:34:19.480
Oh, lady.

788
00:34:20.140 --> 00:34:21.280
Also fascinating.

789
00:34:21.420 --> 00:34:24.340
Who knew these Googles would be so interesting?

790
00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:31.000
The earliest known pie chart is generally credited to William Playfair in 1801.

791
00:34:31.680 --> 00:34:36.020
He was a businessman, engineer, and economics writer from Scotland.

792
00:34:37.240 --> 00:34:38.740
But get this.

793
00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:39.320
What?

794
00:34:39.860 --> 00:34:46.480
Florence Nightingale is credited with adapting the pie chart and basically making it go viral.

795
00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:48.420
Did she use it for pain?

796
00:34:48.580 --> 00:34:50.240
Like, what's your threshold for pain?

797
00:34:50.680 --> 00:34:51.040
No.

798
00:34:51.260 --> 00:34:55.740
But it's interesting you ask that because I will remind people that Florence Nightingale

799
00:34:55.740 --> 00:34:57.960
is the woman who pioneered the field of nursing.

800
00:34:57.960 --> 00:34:59.660
She started a nursing school.

801
00:35:00.400 --> 00:35:05.460
She tended to wounded soldiers during the Crimean War.

802
00:35:06.180 --> 00:35:12.160
But she was also a pioneer in the area of statistics, and she made a pie chart that showed the impact

803
00:35:12.160 --> 00:35:15.880
of poor sanitary conditions on mortality rates.

804
00:35:16.760 --> 00:35:22.000
And some people believe that this is the first time that a pie chart was used to persuade people

805
00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:22.740
to change.

806
00:35:23.320 --> 00:35:24.320
Oh, wow.

807
00:35:24.760 --> 00:35:26.980
And it kind of went viral.

808
00:35:27.420 --> 00:35:32.080
So she's credited as, you know, making the pie chart accessible.

809
00:35:32.840 --> 00:35:33.720
There you go.

810
00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:34.800
There you go.

811
00:35:34.800 --> 00:35:39.340
Well, I looked at Jim's pie chart, took a picture of it, and you know what I thought?

812
00:35:39.820 --> 00:35:40.340
What?

813
00:35:40.600 --> 00:35:42.140
He was just procrastinating.

814
00:35:43.620 --> 00:35:46.940
I mean, really, how different would Jim's pie chart be from Michael's?

815
00:35:47.120 --> 00:35:49.100
The biggest part is in green.

816
00:35:49.100 --> 00:35:50.460
It's procrastinating.

817
00:35:50.720 --> 00:35:54.920
Then the other biggest part is yellow, and it's distracting others.

818
00:35:55.240 --> 00:35:58.420
And then there's a tiny sliver of red for critical thinking.

819
00:35:59.240 --> 00:36:01.000
I mean, Jim's wouldn't be that different.

820
00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:02.400
He's always distracted.

821
00:36:02.400 --> 00:36:03.660
He's procrastinating.

822
00:36:03.700 --> 00:36:04.440
Come on.

823
00:36:04.800 --> 00:36:06.200
You're so right.

824
00:36:06.520 --> 00:36:06.820
Mm-hmm.

825
00:36:06.980 --> 00:36:09.160
Jim just drew his own pie chart.

826
00:36:09.360 --> 00:36:10.040
Pretty much.

827
00:36:10.520 --> 00:36:16.640
While Jim's making pie charts, Pam is still trying to hustle up some money as wedding gifts

828
00:36:16.640 --> 00:36:19.740
and finally finds someone who's going to write her a check.

829
00:36:20.120 --> 00:36:20.700
Kevin!

830
00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:29.640
He writes her a check for $40, and he says he's going to put to love's eternal glory in the subject line.

831
00:36:29.920 --> 00:36:32.820
But also, you know, can you wait until you cash it till Monday?

832
00:36:33.480 --> 00:36:34.100
Yes.

833
00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:41.200
But this is the moment that Pam sees the name Mrs. Pam Halpert for the first time.

834
00:36:41.720 --> 00:36:42.020
Aw.

835
00:36:42.400 --> 00:36:43.760
She gets all excited.

836
00:36:43.760 --> 00:36:45.380
And I have a question.

837
00:36:45.840 --> 00:36:50.640
Pam is doing her talking head out in the little, I don't know, what would you call that?

838
00:36:50.780 --> 00:36:52.140
The vestibule?

839
00:36:52.460 --> 00:36:57.760
The inner lobby from right outside the door of Dunder Mifflin?

840
00:36:58.100 --> 00:36:58.660
Oh, yeah.

841
00:36:58.800 --> 00:36:59.700
Like the entryway.

842
00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:00.380
Yeah.

843
00:37:00.940 --> 00:37:07.020
That Dunder Mifflin sign that Pam is standing next to, isn't that the one that John, like,

844
00:37:07.180 --> 00:37:08.960
took and you saw him putting it in his trunk?

845
00:37:09.200 --> 00:37:09.500
Yeah.

846
00:37:09.700 --> 00:37:10.260
At the end.

847
00:37:10.440 --> 00:37:12.640
That's the one John stole when we wrapped.

848
00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:14.020
That's what I thought.

849
00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:16.480
I was like, oh, hey, John's got that at his house.

850
00:37:16.660 --> 00:37:16.880
Yeah.

851
00:37:18.380 --> 00:37:21.980
Well, listen, I think we should take a break because when we come back,

852
00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:27.760
Jim and Michael are going to try a lot of strategies to divide up this money.

853
00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:30.140
And pretty much none of them are going to go well.

854
00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:36.620
You mean putting beans on people's faces isn't a great way to figure out if they get a raise?

855
00:37:37.480 --> 00:37:39.380
We'll talk about it after the break.

856
00:37:39.720 --> 00:37:40.180
We will.

857
00:37:50.300 --> 00:37:57.180
Jim and Michael are in the conference room and Jim proposes that they give the raises to the salespeople.

858
00:37:57.840 --> 00:38:02.400
He said they're the ones who bring in the money and this will keep them happy.

859
00:38:03.220 --> 00:38:05.960
Well, Michael doesn't think that's going to go over very well.

860
00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:09.020
Well, these guys are going to kind of argue now.

861
00:38:09.020 --> 00:38:12.180
And I thought the scene was a little bit dramatic.

862
00:38:12.180 --> 00:38:14.240
And even Jen Salata mentioned it.

863
00:38:14.440 --> 00:38:15.740
Here's what she had to say.

864
00:38:16.560 --> 00:38:19.340
When I was directing a scene with Steve and John in the conference room,

865
00:38:19.440 --> 00:38:24.240
I think it was the scene where Michael criticizes Jim's way of thinking and Jim tells Michael that he has weaknesses.

866
00:38:24.680 --> 00:38:28.780
I kind of popped out of the moment and looked at us and said, I think we're doing a drama right now.

867
00:38:28.780 --> 00:38:36.660
It occurred to me that I wrote it kind of dramatic and their instincts were dramatic as was fitting for the scene and I was steering it in an even more dramatic way.

868
00:38:37.080 --> 00:38:40.380
I remember trying to push things to their edges a bit more in the sixth season,

869
00:38:40.660 --> 00:38:45.520
maybe as a reaction to having done so many episodes in a row fifth season that I was looking to shake things up a little.

870
00:38:45.520 --> 00:38:48.120
And drama is definitely where some of those scenes went.

871
00:38:48.400 --> 00:38:51.260
Maybe with me writing and directing, there wasn't enough quality control.

872
00:38:51.480 --> 00:38:53.760
I think I even asked Paul, like, is this too dramatic?

873
00:38:54.000 --> 00:38:55.000
Did I make this into a drama?

874
00:38:55.440 --> 00:39:01.320
But then John and Steve and I paused and talked about the balance and landed in a really nice place, I think.

875
00:39:01.320 --> 00:39:04.660
Like, I love what Steve and John did in that scene and throughout that episode.

876
00:39:04.840 --> 00:39:08.760
It was really fun getting to dig into the relationship in a deeper way.

877
00:39:09.860 --> 00:39:11.500
Yeah, I can feel that in this scene.

878
00:39:11.640 --> 00:39:14.200
I mean, Jim's like, you know what, Michael, here are your weaknesses.

879
00:39:14.780 --> 00:39:17.000
And Michael's like, oh, yeah, you want to tell me my weaknesses?

880
00:39:17.160 --> 00:39:18.440
Like, it gets really personal.

881
00:39:18.640 --> 00:39:21.540
Not to mention Michael goes there with the crusty eyes.

882
00:39:22.120 --> 00:39:22.420
Yes.

883
00:39:23.620 --> 00:39:29.680
I really like it when our show would dare to kind of let comedy go for a second.

884
00:39:30.140 --> 00:39:32.260
Sometimes it was in those romantic moments.

885
00:39:32.460 --> 00:39:38.840
Sometimes it was emotional with all the stuff about sprinkles, all the stuff about your heart getting broken, Angela.

886
00:39:39.300 --> 00:39:47.160
Like, I liked it when the show didn't need to be funny or ring that funny bell every three seconds, which is kind of your traditional comedy.

887
00:39:47.360 --> 00:39:49.200
So I really appreciated this scene.

888
00:39:49.400 --> 00:39:50.340
I did, too.

889
00:39:50.560 --> 00:39:53.600
And, of course, they found, like, the moments of comedy.

890
00:39:53.840 --> 00:39:58.580
And one of the lines that made me laugh was at the very end when, you know, Jim is going to go out there.

891
00:39:58.580 --> 00:40:00.500
Michael's like, okay, Skippy, give it your best shot.

892
00:40:00.620 --> 00:40:01.160
Go tell him.

893
00:40:01.760 --> 00:40:02.760
And Jim leaves.

894
00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:06.240
And then Michael turns to Cameron and says, this had better be terrible.

895
00:40:06.680 --> 00:40:06.980
Yeah.

896
00:40:07.660 --> 00:40:09.720
He's rooting for him to fail.

897
00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:14.080
And when Jim does just lay it out there, he's honeSt.

898
00:40:14.240 --> 00:40:15.740
He's going to shoot everyone straight.

899
00:40:15.960 --> 00:40:17.520
And right away, it doesn't go well.

900
00:40:17.980 --> 00:40:19.820
Well, he comes off condescending.

901
00:40:20.080 --> 00:40:25.160
He's talking about, I'm going to talk to you all like adults because you're very smart.

902
00:40:25.600 --> 00:40:25.960
Yes.

903
00:40:26.300 --> 00:40:29.360
And then Oscar says, thank you, Jim, for thinking that we're smart people.

904
00:40:29.660 --> 00:40:33.800
And then Angela, sort of playing dumb, goes, but I do not understand.

905
00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:36.580
If we're such smart adults, then why don't we get raises?

906
00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:39.300
And Oscar's like, excellent question, genius.

907
00:40:39.820 --> 00:40:45.720
Also, by the way, if you go back and rewatch this, tell me if you think I sound Southern when I say, I do not understand.

908
00:40:46.080 --> 00:40:48.000
Because it's all I could hear was my accent.

909
00:40:48.640 --> 00:40:51.500
But anyway, so the scene starts to really get out of hand.

910
00:40:51.620 --> 00:40:53.160
And it builds with this crescendo.

911
00:40:53.300 --> 00:40:54.340
You've got to hear it.

912
00:40:54.740 --> 00:40:56.720
We work just as hard as the salespeople.

913
00:40:56.880 --> 00:40:57.660
We deserve raises.

914
00:40:57.820 --> 00:40:58.000
Yes.

915
00:40:58.200 --> 00:40:58.420
Yes.

916
00:40:58.600 --> 00:41:00.220
They don't get them if we don't get them.

917
00:41:00.380 --> 00:41:01.860
My kid needs shoes.

918
00:41:02.060 --> 00:41:04.060
You want to tell him he doesn't get shoes?

919
00:41:04.300 --> 00:41:04.480
Yeah.

920
00:41:04.640 --> 00:41:06.060
Her kid needs shoes, Jim.

921
00:41:06.660 --> 00:41:07.320
What the hell?

922
00:41:07.560 --> 00:41:07.980
Come on.

923
00:41:08.780 --> 00:41:09.640
How are you?

924
00:41:09.860 --> 00:41:13.440
I mean, Michael got his wish.

925
00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:14.680
That went terribly.

926
00:41:15.380 --> 00:41:15.780
Terribly.

927
00:41:16.320 --> 00:41:19.000
Jim is going to need to rewind out of that.

928
00:41:19.000 --> 00:41:23.620
He's going to do his best little rewind back into the conference room.

929
00:41:23.740 --> 00:41:27.420
And Pam's face where she's like, oh, no, not rewind, guy.

930
00:41:27.600 --> 00:41:28.840
Not rewind, guy.

931
00:41:29.140 --> 00:41:32.760
I went to the script and that reaction was in the script.

932
00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:37.640
It literally says, Pam is embarrassed for Jim.

933
00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:43.140
Jenna, I don't know if you remember one time when I did a rewind bit, but it killed.

934
00:41:43.760 --> 00:41:45.000
Do you remember?

935
00:41:45.160 --> 00:41:46.440
Yes, I do.

936
00:41:46.440 --> 00:41:53.280
We co-presented at the 57th American Cinema Editors Awards, and Jenna and I announced

937
00:41:53.280 --> 00:41:57.820
the winner of the best edited one-hour series for commercial television.

938
00:41:58.140 --> 00:41:59.660
I did my rewind bit.

939
00:42:00.080 --> 00:42:00.920
Jenna, come on.

940
00:42:01.220 --> 00:42:02.300
It was so good.

941
00:42:02.500 --> 00:42:03.300
It was so good.

942
00:42:03.360 --> 00:42:03.880
Want to hear it?

943
00:42:04.380 --> 00:42:05.960
First of all, we had great banter.

944
00:42:06.100 --> 00:42:07.700
I thought we were great co-presenters.

945
00:42:08.080 --> 00:42:13.420
I thought we should get asked to host a whole award show based on that one appearance.

946
00:42:13.480 --> 00:42:14.560
I really did.

947
00:42:14.560 --> 00:42:18.820
I was like, I was so pleased with us that I was like, I'm sorry.

948
00:42:19.280 --> 00:42:20.760
Give us a call, Emmys.

949
00:42:20.760 --> 00:42:22.620
We need to host the whole shebang.

950
00:42:23.080 --> 00:42:25.000
Or at least present one award.

951
00:42:25.980 --> 00:42:26.380
Yeah.

952
00:42:27.240 --> 00:42:28.400
Here's my editor's bit.

953
00:42:28.620 --> 00:42:28.760
Ready?

954
00:42:29.140 --> 00:42:29.380
Yeah.

955
00:42:29.640 --> 00:42:30.540
So we did this bit.

956
00:42:30.640 --> 00:42:32.060
Jenna, remember you set up how we would walk by?

957
00:42:32.060 --> 00:42:35.060
Yeah, I was like, we are very close with our editors.

958
00:42:35.060 --> 00:42:38.140
We're always walking by their edit bay, and here's what we hear.

959
00:42:38.440 --> 00:42:39.860
And then I jumped in and went,

960
00:42:40.200 --> 00:42:44.180
Zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr.

961
00:42:44.560 --> 00:42:45.440
Zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr, zarr.

962
00:42:46.040 --> 00:42:47.120
It's so good.

963
00:42:47.300 --> 00:42:50.240
And it crushed it.

964
00:42:52.660 --> 00:42:56.820
And you folks listening out there, guess who won the award we presented?

965
00:42:56.820 --> 00:43:01.300
It was none other than the editors for Friday Night Lights.

966
00:43:01.840 --> 00:43:02.500
So cool.

967
00:43:02.700 --> 00:43:03.300
So cool.

968
00:43:03.400 --> 00:43:05.440
And it was a show Jenna and I both loved.

969
00:43:05.580 --> 00:43:06.980
We were so excited to meet them.

970
00:43:07.200 --> 00:43:08.580
That was a really fun night.

971
00:43:08.580 --> 00:43:10.340
I found fancy photos of us.

972
00:43:10.660 --> 00:43:13.240
Oh, I found fancy photos of us from that night.

973
00:43:13.400 --> 00:43:17.440
And then also, Jenna, I meant to bring up your red carpet photos for the promotion.

974
00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:20.580
You were all about the over the shoulder.

975
00:43:20.860 --> 00:43:21.920
I was.

976
00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:23.360
You're like, tonight's the night.

977
00:43:23.540 --> 00:43:26.640
I am going to finally get the over the shoulder look.

978
00:43:26.940 --> 00:43:28.220
Wait, I have to show you.

979
00:43:28.940 --> 00:43:30.120
That's very funny.

980
00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:34.860
You know, we did from time to time, a red carpet would come up and we'd be like,

981
00:43:34.860 --> 00:43:38.440
listen, I'm going to nail this new situation.

982
00:43:38.560 --> 00:43:39.700
I don't know what it would be.

983
00:43:39.780 --> 00:43:43.440
It would be like, I'm going to get the pout right tonight or I'm going to nail an over

984
00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:43.980
the shoulder.

985
00:43:44.380 --> 00:43:47.180
I clearly decided to take on over the shoulder.

986
00:43:47.700 --> 00:43:49.680
The promotion was your night for it.

987
00:43:50.000 --> 00:43:51.640
I'll put it in stories, guys.

988
00:43:51.840 --> 00:43:52.700
I can't wait.

989
00:43:52.900 --> 00:43:57.220
Well, listen, in this whole scene, we did get a fan question from Heather B in Victoria,

990
00:43:57.440 --> 00:44:03.380
British Columbia, Canada, who noticed when Oscar tells the office that Jim is only

991
00:44:03.380 --> 00:44:08.800
giving raises to his friends and the people he sleeps with, Pam turns around in her chair

992
00:44:08.800 --> 00:44:11.140
and mouths something in Oscar's direction.

993
00:44:11.420 --> 00:44:13.620
It's at 13 minutes, 18 seconds.

994
00:44:13.860 --> 00:44:15.380
Jenna, what were you saying?

995
00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:17.160
Oh, my God.

996
00:44:17.440 --> 00:44:18.480
I saw it.

997
00:44:18.920 --> 00:44:26.180
I'm saying Oscar, but they cut my audio and I went to the script and it was not scripted.

998
00:44:26.280 --> 00:44:28.420
So clearly I improvised Oscar.

999
00:44:28.420 --> 00:44:32.180
And when they got in the editing room, they were like, we are not interested in this improvisation.

1000
00:44:32.180 --> 00:44:33.580
We are cutting your audio.

1001
00:44:33.800 --> 00:44:34.840
We're cutting you out.

1002
00:44:36.520 --> 00:44:43.120
So anyway, after Jim rewinds back into the conference room, they still have to make this

1003
00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:43.520
decision.

1004
00:44:44.200 --> 00:44:47.840
Michael suggests maybe they do a merit-based raise.

1005
00:44:48.400 --> 00:44:54.460
And this is going to lead them to putting the Boston baked beans on the photos of the

1006
00:44:54.460 --> 00:44:55.100
employees.

1007
00:44:55.760 --> 00:45:01.340
But first, we're going to hear from the largest talking head in office history.

1008
00:45:01.760 --> 00:45:02.820
Six people.

1009
00:45:03.100 --> 00:45:05.320
It's at 14 minutes, 45 seconds.

1010
00:45:05.560 --> 00:45:09.240
There is sort of a pyramid shape of office co-workers.

1011
00:45:09.820 --> 00:45:10.660
Creed at the top.

1012
00:45:10.800 --> 00:45:13.360
In the back row, Meredith, Kevin, and Angela.

1013
00:45:13.580 --> 00:45:15.360
In the front row, Dwight and Oscar.

1014
00:45:16.080 --> 00:45:17.900
And they are not happy.

1015
00:45:18.260 --> 00:45:20.880
Meredith says, I am so pissed at this company.

1016
00:45:21.220 --> 00:45:21.740
Yeah.

1017
00:45:21.740 --> 00:45:23.480
And then Dwight goes, and Jim.

1018
00:45:24.280 --> 00:45:25.720
And Meredith said, who says that?

1019
00:45:25.940 --> 00:45:27.660
